--  Patch shenanigans --

1. On Windows at least, it may be best to not use redirection when
executing patch.  They could work perfectly, but I'm still suspecious
of them.
2. I suggest, when trying to get it to work, using the --dry-run
switch, which will go through the motions of patching without actually
making any changes.  Once you have a dry run that completes without
error, you know you have a good command line.  (Of course, after any
failed patch attempt that's not a dry-run, you'll have to reset your
source tree to a known-good version.  It will probably help to have a
backup copy of such a tree on-hand for ready restoration.)
3. Most uses of patch use the -p option to convert the original
directory paths into something that will fit your system.  -p1 is a
common value, but sometimes you'll use -p0 (which is not the same thing
as not specifying -p at all).
4. Some versions of patch for Windows (especially the GNU version,
oddly) don't like Unix-style line endings, and will produce
difficult-to-track-down errors if some of the files involved has them.
Try opening the patch file in Wordpad and saving it, that'll convert
them to DOS-style.

Hope this has been helpful.  Do you need an explicit command line?  (I
would have supplied one, but I'm having trouble getting it from my
notes right now and I don't have it memorized.)

